    "content": "In Saint-Ouen-sur-Seine, a Paris suburb, the opening of a halal rotisserie chicken shop has triggered a political controversy that has drawn national attention. The restaurant, Master Poulet, began operating in the town square as part of a low-cost halal chicken chain, but soon faced opposition from the local mayor, Socialist Karim Bouamrane.\n\nMayor Bouamrane attempted to block the establishment, citing complaints from residents about smell and noise. However, a court ruled against the mayor, allowing the shop to continue operating. The decision did not quell the dispute, which quickly escalated into a broader national debate.\n\nThe controversy, now referred to in France as l'affaire Master Poulet, has exposed deep divisions within the French left. Two factions have emerged: one supporting the mayor's concerns about local nuisance and secularism, and the other defending the right of the business to operate, viewing the opposition as discriminatory.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora in France and across Europe, this case highlights ongoing tensions around religious accommodation, integration, and local governance. The shop represents a lawful expression of religious dietary practices, and its treatment has become a symbol of broader societal debates.\n\nThe affair underscores how seemingly local issues—like a halal chicken shop—can become flashpoints for national discussions on secularism, minority rights, and identity. It also demonstrates the legal protections available for businesses serving religious communities.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/07/27/a-halal-chicken-shop-that-shook-french-politics/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=a-halal-chicken-shop-that-shook-french-politics",
