    "content": "A dispute has emerged between the regional administrations of Puntland and Galmudug over the movement of Somali federal troops near the contested city of Galkayo. Puntland issued a warning to Galmudug, urging it to refrain from permitting federal forces to pass through Galmudug-controlled areas into Puntland, claiming such actions could destabilize the region.\n\nGalmudug responded by denying the accusations, stating that Puntland was misdirecting blame. The administration advised Puntland to address internal challenges rather than externalize grievances. Galmudug also noted that federal forces do not need to traverse its territory to reach Puntland, as aircraft can land directly at airports within Puntland.\n\nThis disagreement underscores long-standing tensions between the two federal member states, which have competing claims over parts of the Mudug region, particularly Galkayo. The city is divided into sectors controlled by each administration, and sporadic clashes have occurred in the past.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development highlights persistent governance challenges affecting stability in central Somalia. Reliable coordination between regional states is crucial for security and economic activity, especially for diaspora members investing or maintaining ties in the area.\n\nThe situation calls for dialogue to prevent escalation. Both administrations have previously engaged in mediation efforts, but trust remains fragile. Observers emphasize the need for federal government involvement to mediate the dispute.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/07/27/khilaafka-buntilaan-iyo-galmudug-iyo-saamaynta-uu-ku-yeelan-karo-nabadda-gaalkacyo/",
