    "slug": "galmudug-officials-deny-accusations-from-puntland-regarding-arms-transfers",
    "summary": "Galmudug has rejected claims by Puntland that weapons were moved through its territory to attack Puntland regions. The denial comes amid tensions between the two Somali federal member states.",
    "content": "The administration of Galmudug has formally denied allegations made by Puntland that heavy weaponry was transported through Galmudug's airports to be used in attacks on Puntland's territory. In a statement to the BBC Somali Service, Galmudug's Minister of Planning, Investment, and Economic Development, Abdirahman Ahmed Hayle, described the accusations as baseless. He asserted that no arms have been transferred from Galmudug to any area for use against Puntland.\n\nHayle emphasized that relations between Galmudug and Puntland have been characterized by cooperation and mutual respect across multiple sectors. He dismissed the allegations as an attempt to create discord between the two administrations. The minister did not specify which regions or airports were implicated in the original claims.\n\nPuntland officials had earlier asserted that weapons used in recent attacks on its territory originated from or transit through Galmudug-controlled areas. The accusations have heightened political tensions in central Somalia, where both states have overlapping claims and territorial disputes. No independent verification of the claims has been made.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this dispute underscores the fragile nature of inter-state relations within Somalia's federal system. The diaspora community, which often supports stability and reconstruction efforts, may view such disagreements as obstacles to the country's progress. Clear and transparent communication between federal member states is essential to maintaining trust and cooperation.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/07/27/galmudug-oo-beenisay-eedeymo-uga-yimid-puntland/",
